# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECRETS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These values are the credentials your services use to authenticate with
# each other. They are defined here — in a .env file — so that no password
# is ever hardcoded directly in docker-compose.yaml or application code.
#
# HOW THIS PATTERN WORKS:
#   .env.example  → committed to Git. Contains placeholder values (like
#                   these). Shows teammates which variables are required
#                   without exposing real credentials.
#
#   .env          → NEVER committed to Git. Each developer creates this
#                   locally by copying .env.example and filling in real
#                   values. Add ".env" to your .gitignore immediately.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS:
#   Hardcoding a password in a file that gets pushed to GitHub exposes it
#   permanently — even if you delete it later, it lives in Git history.
#   Secrets in .env stay on your machine only.
#
# IN PRODUCTION:
#   A .env file is still a plaintext file on disk, which is acceptable for
#   a local lab but not for a real system. Production environments inject
#   secrets via a secret manager (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault,
#   GCP Secret Manager) or the deployment platform itself (e.g. Kubernetes
#   Secrets, Railway, Render). The docker-compose variable substitution
#   syntax — ${VARIABLE} — works identically regardless of how the variable
#   reaches the environment, so the skill you learn here transfers directly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL credentials
POSTGRES_USER=lab_user
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=xxxx
POSTGRES_DB=lab_db

# ClickHouse credentials
CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=xxxx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TIMEZONE CONFIGURATION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This lab simulates a distributed system spanning two African time zones.
#
#   Lagos, Nigeria  — WAT (West Africa Time)  — UTC+1
#   Nairobi, Kenya  — EAT (East Africa Time)  — UTC+3
#
# Services by timezone:
#   UTC+1 (Lagos)   : PostgreSQL, Producer, Consumers
#   UTC+3 (Nairobi) : ClickHouse, Transformer
#   UTC+0           : Kafka Brokers, Kafka Connect (infrastructure is always UTC)
#
# The 2-hour gap between source and warehouse timestamps is intentional.
# It demonstrates a core challenge in distributed data engineering:
# timestamps must carry timezone context, or they become meaningless.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Source system timezone — Lagos, Nigeria (WAT = UTC+1)
TZ_LAGOS=Africa/Lagos

# Warehouse timezone — Nairobi, Kenya (EAT = UTC+3)
TZ_NAIROBI=Africa/Nairobi

# Infrastructure timezone — Kafka brokers and Kafka Connect always run UTC
TZ_INFRA=UTC
